  [1mMy recommendation:[0m minimal. The clock is the only change source, so [38;5;153mdueAt[39m is the only field notifications actually
  read. A global default lead time ("remind me 30 minutes before") covers most of what per-task settings buy, at a
  fraction of the UI. Adding [38;5;153mremindAt[39m later is an additive field — that's the one migration that stays cheap.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How much should the task model carry in this first version? → Minimal (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the core behavior: what actually fires a reminder.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how many times a single task can notify you, which determines whether the stored task needs to remember what
  it already fired. Once a task can fire more than one distinct reminder, each task carries per-reminder state so a
  refresh doesn't re-fire what you already saw.

  - [1mAt due time only[0m — one notification per task, ever. One [38;5;153mnotifiedAt[39m timestamp and you're done. Simplest, but a
    reminder that arrives exactly when something is due is often too late to act on.
  - [1mLead time, then due[0m — fires once at [38;5;153mdueAt − leadTime[39m, once at [38;5;153mdueAt[39m. Two flags per task. Covers the realistic case:
    warning, then the deadline.
  - [1mLead, due, then overdue nagging[0m — keeps reminding at intervals until you complete or dismiss it. Most likely to
    actually get a task done; also the most likely to be annoying, and it needs dismissal/snooze semantics to be
    tolerable.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m lead time, then due. The warning is the notification that has real value, and stopping at the
  deadline avoids building snooze and nag-suppression UI in v1. Overdue nagging is additive later — it reads the same
  fields.
